    <body>
        <segment id="1" parent="28" relname="concession">As important as it is to maintain consistency in interface design,</segment>
        <segment id="2" parent="28" relname="span">it is also important to avoid unnecessary consistency.</segment>
        <segment id="3" parent="29" relname="span">Scott Berkun (1999) of the Microsoft Corporation believes that consistence can become a self - perpetuating monster which can serve no benefit to the end user.</segment>
        <segment id="4" parent="30" relname="span">In other words, what may work for one, may not work for another.</segment>
        <segment id="5" parent="31" relname="span">Berkun (1999) equates this to interface design for video games.</segment>
        <segment id="6" parent="33" relname="contrast">The best user interface for a driving game would be a steering wheel,</segment>
        <segment id="7" parent="33" relname="contrast">while the best interface for Pac-Man, would be a joystick.</segment>
        <segment id="8" parent="33" relname="nonvolitional-result">In this case, it is better to ignore the consistence rules and customize the interface according to the tasks that will be performed.</segment>
        <segment id="9" parent="34" relname="span">In other instance, however, consistency allows users to transfer knowledge from on e application to another (Hewlett Packard, 2003).</segment>
        <segment id="10" parent="9" relname="elaboration">Consistency creates ease of learning, ease of use, and reduction of errors as well as prevents confusion for users while they work with a variety of applications at the same time.</segment>
        <segment id="11" parent="35" relname="span">According to Burkin (1999), there are several rules of thumb for consistency:</segment>
        <segment id="12" parent="23" relname="list">1.	Begin by using existing controls or UI concepts in sketches and prototypes.</segment>
        <segment id="13" parent="14" relname="condition">2.	If sketches and prototypes are not working in user tests or other evaluations because of the failure of existing concepts,</segment>
        <segment id="14" parent="24" relname="span">the existing concept should be expanded to cover the new situation that has arisen.</segment>
        <segment id="15" parent="16" relname="condition">3.	If the concept can not be extended,</segment>
        <segment id="16" parent="25" relname="span">a new control or concept needs to be designed in order to solve the problem.</segment>
        <segment id="17" parent="18" relname="condition">4.	If special cases are to be used,</segment>
        <segment id="18" parent="26" relname="span">it is important to ensure the best trade-off is received.</segment>
        <segment id="19" parent="23" relname="summary">Burkin (1999) believes these guidelines will help designers be as consistent as possible while avoiding foolish consistency.</segment>
        <group id="20" type="span"/>
